{
  "id": "ep093",
  "duration": 44.402,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.084,
      "text": "Think the Moon doesn't spin? That is a total myth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Bill Anders",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 3.1,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.084,
      "end": 9.079,
      "text": "The Moon actually rotates on its axis at the exact same speed it orbits our home planet.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Bill Anders",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.1,
          "end": 9.067,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 9.079,
      "end": 14.374,
      "text": "This cosmic synchronization keeps the exact same side facing Earth forever.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Tidal bulge.png",
        "credit": "Orion 8",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tidal_bulge.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 9.067,
          "end": 14.367,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 14.374,
      "end": 21.544,
      "text": "Earth's immense gravity constantly stretches the Moon into tidal bulges that generate gravitational torque.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wellcome Collection",
        "title": "Astronomy: eclipses (top), and the Moon's passage around the Earth. Coloured engraving by J. Emslie, 1851, after himself",
        "credit": "Wellcome Collection",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://wellcomecollection.org/works/dmzeqfcb"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 14.367,
          "end": 17.2,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 17.2,
          "end": 21.533,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 21.544,
      "end": 29.789,
      "text": "In 1959, the Soviet spacecraft Luna 3 successfully transmitted the very first photographs of the far side.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Luna-3 (Memorial Museum of Astronautics).JPG",
        "credit": "Armael",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luna-3_(Memorial_Museum_of_Astronautics).JPG"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 21.533,
          "end": 24.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 24.667,
          "end": 29.8,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 29.789,
      "end": 37.214,
      "text": "This tidal locking is precisely why humanity never caught a single glimpse of that hidden hemisphere before.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Moon PIA00304.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moon_PIA00304.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 29.8,
          "end": 33.5,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 33.5,
          "end": 37.2,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 37.214,
      "end": 44.052,
      "text": "Because tidal locking ensures the Moon's rotation matches its orbit, you have to wonder if it ever stays still.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Bill Anders",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 37.2,
          "end": 44.4,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
